/// \defgroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c Update modules and their factory functions (InvisibilityUpdate to RainOfFireUpdate)
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Sixteen of BFME 2's update module names, from InvisibilityUpdate
///        to RainOfFireUpdate: the instance factories twelve of them
///        register, and the data factories of twelve module data classes.
///
/// Each module here is attached to an object by its `Object` block in INI
/// (\ref page_modding_modules). This group documents how each module and
/// its data come into existence, the class each module is built on, and
/// the INI fields its data accepts. It does not describe what the modules
/// do every frame. BFME 2 registers many other update modules; only the
/// classes listed below are covered here. For InvisibilityUpdate,
/// LargeGroupAudioUpdate, LargeGroupBonusUpdate and PartTheHeavensUpdate
/// only the instance factory is documented here, and for LaserUpdate,
/// OCLUpdate, ProductionUpdate and RadarUpdate only the data factory; the
/// class pages say so too.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. Every module
/// name here is spelled like its class.
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads.
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the data class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object is built.
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories walks through this flow
/// step by step; it applies here unchanged. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers, so no direct caller is known. Which class a factory belongs to
/// is established by the ModuleFactory registration that pairs it with a
/// module name.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from that
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as
///   a C++ new-expression requires. When the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Every data factory documented here has the same frame and calls
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc after construction, outside the exception
///   frame, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new
///   data. The bodies test only the INI pointer before that call, not the
///   new data pointer.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par How the INI tables combine
/// A data class's \c buildFieldParse hands INI::initFromINIMultiProc one or
/// more field tables, a base class's first. Read from the matched bodies:
/// MultiIniFieldParse::add appends each table to a fixed list of sixteen
/// and throws when the list is full, and INI::initFromINIMulti looks each
/// field name up table by table, in that order, and uses the first match.
/// A name that no table knows raises an INI error ("Unknown field")
/// instead of being skipped, so a misspelled field name is reported. When
/// two tables in one list share a name, only the earlier one is reachable;
/// MissileUpdateModuleData is the case in this group.
///
/// \par Module types and interfaces
/// The registration's module type and interface mask are read here by
/// their Zero Hour meanings (`Common/Module.h`); this page does not
/// confirm that BFME 2 uses the same values for the same things.
/// - Fifteen names are registered with MODULETYPE_BEHAVIOR, the logic
///   modules that Zero Hour attaches with an object's \c Behavior lines.
/// - LaserUpdate alone is registered with the type Zero Hour calls
///   MODULETYPE_CLIENT_UPDATE and the single interface bit Zero Hour calls
///   MODULEINTERFACE_CLIENT_UPDATE. BFME 2 registers the same type and
///   mask for AnimatedParticleSysBoneClientUpdate, SwayClientUpdate,
///   BeaconClientUpdate, RadarMarkerClientUpdate and
///   EvaAnnounceClientCreate. See LaserUpdateModuleData.
/// - Among the behavior names, the masks add bits to MODULEINTERFACE_UPDATE
///   as follows: MissileUpdate adds MODULEINTERFACE_COLLIDE, the same mask
///   BezierProjectileBehavior has; OneRingPenaltyUpdate and ProductionUpdate
///   add MODULEINTERFACE_DIE, as Zero Hour's ProductionUpdate does;
///   RadiateFearUpdate adds MODULEINTERFACE_UPGRADE. The other eleven carry
///   the update bit alone.
///
/// \par Base classes, read from the retail constructors
/// - InvisibilityUpdate, LargeGroupAudioUpdate, LargeGroupBonusUpdate,
///   MonitorConditionUpdate, OneRingPenaltyUpdate, PartTheHeavensUpdate,
///   PickupStuffUpdate, RadiateFearUpdate and RainOfFireUpdate start their
///   constructors by running UpdateModule's constructor, so they are plain
///   update modules. RadiateFearUpdate's constructor also runs UpgradeMux's
///   constructor, which fits its upgrade bit; Zero Hour: UpgradeMux is the
///   part through which a module responds to upgrades.
/// - MissileUpdate runs BezierProjectileBehavior's constructor,
///   MonsterDockUpdate runs DockUpdate's and OilSpillUpdate runs
///   FireWeaponUpdate's; each data class's \c buildFieldParse starts with
///   the matching base table (see the class pages).
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has LaserUpdate, OCLUpdate, ProductionUpdate and RadarUpdate;
/// the LaserUpdate, OCLUpdate and ProductionUpdate field tables differ
/// from BFME 2's as noted on the class pages, while RadarUpdate's single
/// field is the same.
/// Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources for LargeGroupAudioUpdate,
/// LargeGroupBonusUpdate, LaserUpdate, MonsterDockUpdate, OCLUpdate,
/// OneRingPenaltyUpdate, PartTheHeavensUpdate, ProductionUpdate,
/// RadarUpdate and RainOfFireUpdate. InvisibilityUpdate, MissileUpdate,
/// MonitorConditionUpdate, OilSpillUpdate, PickupStuffUpdate and
/// RadiateFearUpdate appear in neither. All INI field names on these pages
/// are read from the retail executable's own field tables.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, and every
///   field on the class pages.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, their base classes and what each class
///   does with its fields. A new kind of update needs a new class and a new
///   ModuleFactory::init registration.
/// - Multiplayer: the fifteen behavior modules run in the game logic, and
///   their data comes from `Object` blocks, so every machine must load
///   identical values (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - LargeGroupBonusUpdate draws one logic random number in its constructor
///   (see its class page). Adding it to an object, or removing it, changes
///   how many logic random numbers are drawn whenever such an object is
///   built, so every later draw shifts and replays recorded before the
///   change no longer play back the same way
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_random).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Duration fields parsed by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt are turned from
/// milliseconds into whole logic frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details);
/// INI::parseDurationReal scales by the same milliseconds-to-frames factor
/// and keeps the fraction. Defaults set by a data constructor are stored
/// as they are, already in frames. Fields read with INI::parseReal, such as
/// PickupStuffUpdateModuleData's \c ScanIntervalSeconds and
/// LaserUpdateModuleData's \c LaserLifetime, are not converted at all, so
/// their unit is whatever the consuming code expects. A change to the
/// logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore affects these kinds of
/// field differently, and breaks play with unmodified clients and replays.
///
/// \see \ref sub_objects_modules, \ref sub_rts_model_flow,
///      \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories


/// \class InvisibilityUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Update module registered as "InvisibilityUpdate".
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor first, so it is a plain
/// update module. Its registration pairs it with
/// InvisibilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, which this group
/// does not document. Neither Zero Hour nor Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has
/// the class, and what the module does is not described here.
///
/// \see InvisibilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *InvisibilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "InvisibilityUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by InvisibilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see InvisibilityUpdate


/// \class LargeGroupAudioUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Update module registered as "LargeGroupAudioUpdate".
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor first, so it is a plain
/// update module. Its registration pairs it with
/// LargeGroupAudioUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, which this group
/// does not document; that data's table, read from retail, holds
/// \c TimeBetweenUpdatesMin and \c TimeBetweenUpdatesVariation
/// (durations), \c UnitWeight and \c Key. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class;
/// Zero Hour does not. What the module does is not described here.
///
/// \see LargeGroupAudioUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *LargeGroupAudioUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "LargeGroupAudioUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by
///                    LargeGroupAudioUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see LargeGroupAudioUpdate


/// \class LargeGroupBonusUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Update module registered as "LargeGroupBonusUpdate", whose first
///        update waits a random number of logic frames.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor first, so it is a plain
/// update module. It then delays the module's first update by a random
/// number of logic frames, drawn by GetGameLogicRandomValue between 1 and
/// the data's \c UpdateRate. UpdateModule::setWakeFrame adds that delay to
/// the current logic frame. The file name the random call passes for
/// debugging names the
/// original source file LargeGroupBonusUpdate.cpp under
/// `GameLogic/Object/Update`. Spreading first updates this way keeps many
/// such objects from all updating on the same frame (an inference from
/// the code, not a stated design).
///
/// Its registration pairs it with
/// LargeGroupBonusUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, which this group
/// does not document. That data's table, read from retail, holds
/// \c UpdateRate (a duration), \c HordeMemberFilter, \c Count, \c Radius,
/// \c RubOffRadius, \c AlliesOnly, \c FlagSubObjectNames and
/// \c AttributeModifier. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class; Zero
/// Hour does not.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The random draw comes from the logic random stream, so it is the same
/// on every machine of a game; a mod that adds or removes this module
/// shifts every later logic random draw (see the group page).
///
/// \see LargeGroupBonusUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *LargeGroupBonusUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "LargeGroupBonusUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern. The constructor it runs draws one logic
/// random number (see the class page).
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by
///                    LargeGroupBonusUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see LargeGroupBonusUpdate


/// \class LaserUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Module data for "LaserUpdate": particle systems for the muzzle
///        and the target, a firing bone and a lifetime.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// LaserUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every object
/// of the template. Its constructor leaves the names empty, the bone flag
/// false and the lifetime zero. Its \c buildFieldParse registers the
/// class's one table and no base table.
///
/// "LaserUpdate" is the one module in this group not registered with the
/// behavior module type (see the group page). In BFME 2,
/// W3DLaserDraw::doDrawModule looks up a client update module named
/// \c LaserUpdate on its drawable and draws nothing without one
/// (\ref grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories). Together these suggest
/// that, as in Zero Hour, where LaserUpdate derives from ClientUpdateModule
/// and an object lists it on a \c ClientUpdate line, the module belongs to
/// the drawable rather than to the logic object. That reading is not
/// confirmed here: the BFME 2 LaserUpdate class and the INI key that
/// attaches it are not documented in this group.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c MuzzleParticleSystem,
/// \c TargetParticleSystem and \c ParentFireBoneName (names, stored as
/// text), \c ParentFireBoneOnTurret (boolean) and \c LaserLifetime (a real
/// number, not converted to frames). Zero Hour's table has the two
/// particle system fields and \c PunchThroughScalar, which is not in this
/// table.
///
/// The instance factory registered for "LaserUpdate" is not documented in
/// this group.
///
/// \see LaserUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *LaserUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "LaserUpdate": builds the module data
///        for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs LaserUpdateModuleData
/// with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and LaserUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see LaserUpdateModuleData


/// \class MissileUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Projectile module registered as "MissileUpdate": a
///        BezierProjectileBehavior with fuel, ignition and diving
///        settings.
///
/// Its constructor runs BezierProjectileBehavior's constructor first, then
/// sets up members of its own, among them a record of the current logic
/// frame. Its registration carries the same module type and interface
/// mask as BezierProjectileBehavior's, and its data class builds on
/// BezierProjectileBehaviorModuleData, so MissileUpdate is a specialised
/// Bezier projectile (a structural inference from the retail bodies).
///
/// Neither Zero Hour nor Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class. Zero
/// Hour's MissileAIUpdate, an AI update, has fields of the same names as
/// several here (see MissileUpdateModuleData).
///
/// \see MissileUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      MissileUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class MissileUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Module data for MissileUpdate: the Bezier projectile fields plus
///        fuel, ignition, turning and diving settings.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// MissileUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every object
/// of the template. Its constructor runs BezierProjectileBehaviorModuleData's
/// constructor first. Its \c buildFieldParse calls
/// BezierProjectileBehaviorModuleData::buildFieldParse and then adds its
/// own table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own INI fields, read from the retail table: \c FuelLifetime and
/// \c IgnitionDelay (durations), \c DistanceToTravelBeforeTurning and
/// \c DistanceToTargetBeforeDiving (reals), \c IgnitionFX (an FX list),
/// \c DetonateOnNoFuel (boolean) and \c ExhaustTemplate (a name, stored as
/// text). The base table adds the Bezier flight, bounce and landing fields
/// (for example \c FirstHeight, \c BounceCount, \c GroundHitFX and
/// \c PreLandingEmotion).
/// - The own table also lists \c GarrisonHitKillRequiredKindOf,
///   \c GarrisonHitKillForbiddenKindOf, \c GarrisonHitKillCount and
///   \c GarrisonHitKillFX, but the base table, searched first, has fields
///   of the same four names (see "How the INI tables combine" on the group
///   page). A line with one of those names therefore sets the base class's
///   value, and the same-named fields in this class's own part cannot be
///   set from INI. Which copy the missile code reads is not established
///   here.
/// - Every field in this class's own table except \c ExhaustTemplate has
///   the same name as a field in Zero Hour's MissileAIUpdate table, which
///   also has further fields of its own. Those shared names are parsed
///   the same way here, except that this page does not identify the
///   parser BFME 2 uses for the two kind-of fields (Zero Hour reads them
///   as sets of object kinds).
///
/// \see MissileUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *MissileUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "MissileUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern; the constructor it runs starts with
/// BezierProjectileBehavior's.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's MissileUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see MissileUpdate, MissileUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *MissileUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "MissileUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs MissileUpdateModuleData
/// with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and MissileUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse,
/// so the block accepts the Bezier projectile fields and the class's own.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see MissileUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, MissileUpdateModuleData


/// \class MonitorConditionUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Update module registered as "MonitorConditionUpdate", configured
///        by model condition flags, weapon set flags and two command sets.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor first, so it is a plain
/// update module. Its data is MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData. Neither
/// Zero Hour nor Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class. The field names
/// suggest it watches the object's model or weapon set condition and
/// switches command sets; that is an inference from the names, and the
/// module's logic is not described here.
///
/// \see MonitorConditionUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Module data for MonitorConditionUpdate: two sets of flags and
///        two command set names.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. Its constructor clears both flag sets and
/// leaves both names empty. Its \c buildFieldParse registers the class's
/// one table and no base table.
///
/// The ModuleFactory registration for "MonitorConditionUpdate" is what
/// names this class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c ModelConditionFlags and
/// \c WeaponSetFlags (sets of named flags), and
/// \c ModelConditionCommandSet and \c WeaponToggleCommandSet (command set
/// names, stored as text). Command sets themselves are defined elsewhere
/// (\ref page_core_frameworks_commands); this page does not establish
/// when, or whether, a misspelled set name is reported.

/// \fn Module *MonitorConditionUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "MonitorConditionUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData for
///                    this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see MonitorConditionUpdate,
///      MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "MonitorConditionUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs
/// MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when \p ini is
/// not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see MonitorConditionUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      MonitorConditionUpdateModuleData


/// \class MonsterDockUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Dock module registered as "MonsterDockUpdate": a DockUpdate
///        configured by an object filter and a docked animation time.
///
/// Its constructor runs DockUpdate's constructor first, and its data class
/// builds on DockUpdateModuleData, so it is a dock. Open-BFME-1's \c game/
/// tree has the class; Zero Hour does not, though Zero Hour has the
/// DockUpdate base with the same two base fields. Zero Hour: a dock
/// reserves approach positions for the objects waiting to use it and
/// serves one active docker at a time. What this subclass does with its
/// filter and docked objects is not described here.
///
/// \see MonsterDockUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      MonsterDockUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class MonsterDockUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Module data for MonsterDockUpdate: the dock fields plus an object
///        filter and a docked animation time.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// MonsterDockUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template. Its constructor runs DockUpdateModuleData's
/// constructor and sets the animation time to zero. Its \c buildFieldParse
/// calls DockUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse and then adds its own table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail tables: from the dock base,
/// \c NumberApproachPositions (whole number) and \c AllowsPassthrough
/// (boolean), the same names as Zero Hour's DockUpdate; own fields,
/// \c DockableObjectFilter (an object filter) and \c DockedAnimationTime
/// (a duration).

/// \fn Module *MonsterDockUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "MonsterDockUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern; the constructor it runs starts with
/// DockUpdate's.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's MonsterDockUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see MonsterDockUpdate, MonsterDockUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *MonsterDockUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "MonsterDockUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs
/// MonsterDockUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not
/// null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// MonsterDockUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, so the block accepts the
/// dock fields and the class's own.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see MonsterDockUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      MonsterDockUpdateModuleData


/// \class OCLUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_c
/// \brief Module data for "OCLUpdate": an object creation list, a delay
///        range, an edge flag and an amount.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// OCLUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every object of
/// the template. Its constructor sets no list, both delays to zero, the
/// edge flag to false and the amount to -1. Its \c buildFieldParse
/// registers the class's one table and no base table. Zero Hour: OCLUpdate
/// runs its object creation list again and again, at the object or, with
/// \c CreateAtEdge, at the nearest map edge point, waiting between runs
/// for a logic random time between the two delays.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c OCL (an object creation list
/// name, resolved by INI::parseObjectCreationList), \c MinDelay and
/// \c MaxDelay (durations), \c CreateAtEdge (boolean) and \c Amount (an
/// integer, default -1; what -1 means to OCLUpdate is not established
/// here). Zero Hour's table has \c FactionOCL and \c FactionTriggered,
/// which are not in this table, and no \c Amount.
///
/// The instance factory registered for "OCLUpdate" is not documented in
/// this group.
///
/// \see OCLUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *OCLUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "OCLUpdate": builds the module data
///        for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
